{
  "summary": "Researched and provided a comprehensive numbered outline about the CursorToys extension (godrix.cursor-toys). Covered all 9 utility categories (Smart Recommendations, Instant Sharing, API Testing, Personal Libraries, Project Notepads, Hooks Management, File Minification, Chat Integration, GitHub Gist Integration), keybindings, developer vs creative writing use cases. Concluded most features are developer-focused with only Personal Libraries and Chat Integration having marginal value for creative writing.",
  "current_status": "Informational query resolved. No changes made to the project. Extension evaluation provided for CursorToys."
}

{
  "summary": "User asked whether COMPASS_MCP_MANIFEST.md or zz_COMPASS/MCP_Implementations/COMPASS_INTELLIGENCE_MCP/ is how to call the COMPASS MCP. Investigated both: the manifest is a reference document (not functional), and the COMPASS Intelligence MCP is a fully built Python MCP server with tools (kg_to_mermaid, extraction, prompt patterns, pipeline) but it is NOT registered in ~/.cursor/mcp.json — meaning Cursor can't see or use it. Listed all 14 MCPs that ARE actually registered and running.",
  "current_status": "COMPASS Intelligence MCP exists as code but is not wired into Cursor. Would need to be added to ~/.cursor/mcp.json to become functional. No changes made to the project."
}

{
  "summary": "User pointed out .cursor/config/mcp.json which contains 50+ MCP definitions including compass-intelligence (enabled, line 419-425). Corrected previous finding — the MCP IS registered, but in .cursor/config/mcp.json rather than the standard .cursor/mcp.json path. This non-standard path may mean Cursor isn't reading it, explaining why only global MCPs from ~/.cursor/mcp.json appear in the runtime mcps/ directory. Key question raised: should this file be moved to .cursor/mcp.json for Cursor to recognize it?",
  "current_status": "Identified potential config path issue — .cursor/config/mcp.json may not be read by Cursor. Awaiting user clarification on whether the file placement was intentional."
}

